  user_input {
    user_response: "# MASTER PROMPT: VELoop Rewards \342\200\223 Complete Full-Stack User Profile & Admin Management System\n\n## 1. Your Role\n\nAct as a senior full-stack MERN developer, UI/UX designer, database architect, security engineer, and technical mentor.\n\nI want you to build a complete, production-quality full-stack web application named **VELoop Rewards \342\200\223 User Profile & Admin Management System**.\n\nThis is a real internship assignment. It must be a functional application, not just a frontend prototype or static dashboard.\n\nYou are responsible for:\n- Planning the architecture.\n- Designing the UI/UX.\n- Building the React frontend.\n- Building the Node.js and Express backend.\n- Designing MongoDB schemas.\n- Implementing authentication and authorization.\n- Connecting all frontend pages to backend APIs.\n- Building a protected Admin Panel.\n- Testing and fixing errors.\n- Deploying the application.\n- Preparing technical documentation.\n\n**Important:** I am a beginner in full-stack development. Write clean, understandable code. Explain what each major file does, why a particular technology is used, and how different components communicate.\n\nDo not generate the entire project in one response. Work in small, manageable phases.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Mandatory Technology Stack\n\nUse the following technologies without replacing them with unrelated platforms.\n\n### Frontend\n- React.js\n- Vite\n- JavaScript\n- Bootstrap\n- CSS Modules (`.module.css`)\n- Lucide React\n- React Icons\n- React Router DOM\n- Axios or Fetch API\n\n### Backend\n- Node.js\n- Express.js\n- JavaScript\n- MongoDB\n- Mongoose\n- JWT-based authentication or an equally secure session-based approach\n- bcrypt for password hashing\n- Express middleware\n- Server-side validation\n\n### Deployment\n- Frontend: Vercel\n- Backend: Render or Railway\n- Database: MongoDB Atlas\n- Source control: GitHub\n\nDo not use Firebase, Supabase, or a static JSON file as a replacement for the required backend and database.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Project Structure\n\nCreate a clean and modular project structure.\n\n```text\nveloop-user-profile/\n\342\224\202\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 frontend/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 public/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 src/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 assets/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 components/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 ProfileHero/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 RewardAssets/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 LevelProgress/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 AchievementCard/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 WithdrawalOverview/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 ReferralSnapshot/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 RecentActivity/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 QuickActions/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 pages/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 UserProfile/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 Login/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 Admin/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202       \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 AdminLogin/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202       \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 Dashboard/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202       \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 Users/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202       \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 Rewards/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202       \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 Withdrawals/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202       \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 Referrals/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202       \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 Levels/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202       \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 Achievements/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202       \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 AuditLogs/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 services/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 context/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 hooks/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 utils/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 App.jsx\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 main.jsx\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202\n\342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 package.json\n\342\224\202\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 backend/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 src/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 config/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 controllers/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 middleware/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 models/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 routes/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 services/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 validators/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 utils/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 server.js\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202\n\342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 package.json\n\342\224\202\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 docs/\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 postman/\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 README.md\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 .gitignore\n\342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 .env.example\n```\n\nYou may improve the structure if necessary, but maintain modularity and separation of concerns.\n\n---\n\n# PART A \342\200\223 USER PROFILE APPLICATION\n\n## 4. User Profile Dashboard\n\nCreate a complete and visually attractive user profile page at:\n\n`/user-profile`\n\nThe profile page should work after refreshing the browser and after logging out and logging back in.\n\nCreate a new design. Do not simply copy an existing profile page.\n\n### A. Profile Hero Section\n\nDisplay:\n- User profile picture.\n- Initials fallback if there is no profile image.\n- Display name.\n- User ID.\n- Email.\n- Account status.\n- Current level.\n- Level name.\n- XP progress bar.\n- Profile settings button, only if the feature exists.\n\nExample layout:\n\n```text\nWelcome back, Alex!\n\nLevel 04 \342\200\223 Reward Explorer\n\n6,420 / 8,000 XP\n\n[Progress Bar]\n\n1,580 XP remaining to reach Level 05\n```\n\nThese are illustrative values only. Never use them as actual user information.\n\n### B. Personal Information\n\nDisplay:\n- User ID.\n- Full name.\n- Email address.\n- Referral code.\n- Account creation date.\n- Account status.\n- Current level.\n\nAdd copy buttons for User ID and Referral Code.\n\nShow a success toast when copied.\n\nNever display passwords, OTPs, JWT tokens, or private credentials.\n\n### C. Reward Assets\n\nCreate an attractive section called **Your Rewards**.\n\nDisplay five separate reward assets:\n\n1. VEs.\n2. SVEs.\n3. Gems.\n4. Tokens.\n5. XP.\n\nEach asset should have:\n- Its own icon.\n- Current balance.\n- Clear label.\n- Short explanation.\n- Tooltip or information icon.\n- Relevant action link if a real feature exists.\n\nUse distinct visual treatments for:\n- VEs.\n- Silver-themed SVEs.\n- Gems.\n- Tokens.\n- XP.\n\nImportant:\n- All balances must come from MongoDB-backed APIs or an approved existing wallet service.\n- Never hardcode balances.\n- XP is progression data, not redeemable wallet currency.\n- Do not invent conversion functionality.\n\n### D. Level Progress\n\nCreate a separate level progression section.\n\nDisplay:\n- Current level.\n- Current level name.\n- Current XP.\n- XP required for next level.\n- Remaining XP.\n- Progress percentage.\n- Next level preview.\n- Badge artwork, if available.\n\nThe backend must calculate and enforce level progression.\n\n### E. Achievements and Badges\n\nDisplay:\n- Unlocked achievements.\n- Achievement names.\n- Badge artwork.\n- Unlock status.\n- Next achievement.\n- Progress toward the next achievement.\n\nUse actual achievement records and backend rules.\n\nIf the platform does not support achievements yet, show an informative upcoming-feature or empty state. Do not invent achievements.\n\n### F. Withdrawal Overview\n\nCreate a withdrawal statistics section.\n\nDisplay:\n- Total withdrawal requests.\n- Pending requests.\n- Approved requests.\n- Rejected requests.\n- Failed requests, if supported.\n- Total successfully withdrawn amount.\n\nUse appropriate status labels:\n- Approved: green.\n- Pending: amber.\n- Rejected/failed: muted red.\n\nNever count pending withdrawals as successfully withdrawn.\n\n### G. Quick Actions\n\nCreate attractive interactive cards for supported actions, such as:\n- Earn More Rewards.\n- Get More Gems.\n- Redeem Rewards.\n- Watch Ads.\n- Refer Friends.\n- View Wallet.\n- Explore Features.\n- View Achievements.\n\nOnly link to real implemented routes or supported platform features.\n\n### H. Referral Snapshot\n\nDisplay:\n- Referral code.\n- Total referrals.\n- Successful referrals.\n- Pending referrals, if supported.\n- Referral rewards earned.\n- Referral XP.\n- Next milestone, if configured.\n\nAdd a copy referral code button.\n\nCalculate referral statistics from actual database records.\n\n### I. Recent Activity\n\nDisplay a list of recent activities, including supported events such as:\n- Reward earned.\n- XP earned.\n- Daily bonus.\n- Gem reward.\n- Referral reward.\n- Redemption request.\n- Completed withdrawal.\n\nEach activity should show:\n- Description.\n- Amount or asset.\n- Timestamp.\n- Status.\n\nUse persisted backend records, not fictional activity.\n\n### J. Account Information and Security\n\nShow:\n- Email.\n- User ID.\n- Member-since date.\n- Account status.\n- Email verification status, if tracked.\n- Relevant security information.\n\nDo not expose sensitive authentication information.\n\n---\n\n# PART B \342\200\223 BACKEND DEVELOPMENT\n\n## 5. Database Architecture\n\nUse MongoDB and Mongoose.\n\nCreate suitable models for:\n\n1. User\n2. Wallet\n3. WalletTransaction\n4. UserLevel / LevelConfiguration\n5. Achievement\n6. UserAchievement\n7. Referral\n8. Withdrawal / Redemption\n9. ActivityLog\n10. AdminUser\n11. AdminAuditLog\n\nDefine appropriate:\n- Schemas.\n- Data types.\n- Required fields.\n- Default values.\n- Validation.\n- Relationships.\n- Indexes.\n- Created and updated timestamps.\n\nAvoid duplicate sources of truth.\n\nFor example, do not create two independent wallet balances for the same user.\n\nIf an existing VELoop wallet service exists, integrate with its approved API rather than creating conflicting wallet data.\n\n## 6. Backend APIs\n\nCreate REST APIs for the user profile.\n\nSuggested endpoints:\n\n```text\nGET    /api/profile/me\nPATCH  /api/profile/me\n\nGET    /api/profile/rewards\nGET    /api/profile/level-progress\nGET    /api/profile/achievements\nGET    /api/profile/withdrawal-summary\nGET    /api/profile/referrals\nGET    /api/profile/activity\n```\n\nCreate authentication endpoints:\n\n```text\nPOST   /api/auth/register\nPOST   /api/auth/login\nPOST   /api/auth/logout\nGET    /api/auth/me\n```\n\nOnly implement registration if appropriate for the demo application.\n\nEvery protected API must:\n- Verify authentication.\n- Identify the current user from the verified session or token.\n- Check permissions.\n- Validate incoming data.\n- Return appropriate HTTP status codes.\n- Handle errors securely.\n\nNever trust a user ID supplied by the frontend to determine account ownership.\n\n## 7. Backend Data Integrity\n\nThe following values must only be controlled by the backend:\n\n- Wallet balances.\n- VEs.\n- SVEs.\n- Gems.\n- Tokens.\n- XP.\n- Level.\n- Achievement unlocks.\n- Withdrawal totals.\n- Referral rewards.\n- Account status.\n- Verification status.\n\nA user must not be able to modify these values through browser DevTools or API requests.\n\nIf users can edit their display name or other permitted profile fields, validate those fields separately.\n\nCreate centralized error handling and request validation.\n\n---\n\n# PART C \342\200\223 ADMIN PANEL\n\n## 8. Protected Admin Dashboard\n\nCreate a separate admin application section.\n\nSuggested routes:\n\n```text\n/admin/login\n/admin/dashboard\n/admin/users\n/admin/users/:id\n/admin/rewards\n/admin/levels\n/admin/achievements\n/admin/withdrawals\n/admin/referrals\n/admin/activity\n/admin/audit-logs\n/admin/settings\n```\n\nBuild a professional admin interface with:\n- Sidebar navigation.\n- Top navigation bar.\n- Search.\n- Filters.\n- Data tables.\n- Statistics cards.\n- Confirmation dialogs.\n- Notifications.\n- Responsive layout.\n\nThe Admin Panel must use real backend data.\n\nIt must not be a static dashboard.\n\n## 9. Admin Authentication\n\nImplement:\n- Admin login.\n- Secure logout.\n- Password hashing using bcrypt.\n- Session or token expiry.\n- Protected routes.\n- Role and permission checks.\n- Login rate limiting.\n- Protection against repeated failed login attempts.\n\nCreate a Super Admin role with authority over the implemented profile module.\n\nNever grant admin privileges based on a frontend role value or user-editable email.\n\n## 10. Demo Admin Credentials\n\nCreate a working development/demo admin account through a secure seed script.\n\nThe demo credentials must:\n- Actually work.\n- Be stored securely.\n- Be available to the reviewer.\n- Be documented in `DEMO_ACCESS.md`.\n\nDisplay a Demo Credentials panel on the admin login page only in the explicitly configured demo environment.\n\nNever expose production admin passwords or secrets in frontend code.\n\n## 11. Admin Dashboard\n\nDisplay actual backend statistics:\n\n- Total registered users.\n- Active users.\n- Suspended users, if supported.\n- Total wallet balances by asset.\n- Total withdrawal requests.\n- Pending withdrawals.\n- Approved withdrawals.\n- Completed withdrawals.\n- Referral statistics.\n- Recent activity.\n- Relevant system alerts.\n\nIf the database is empty, show an empty state rather than fabricated numbers.\n\n## 12. Admin User Management\n\nAllow administrators to:\n- Search users.\n- Filter users by account status.\n- View user profiles.\n- View wallet balances.\n- View transaction history.\n- View level and XP progress.\n- View achievements.\n- View referrals.\n- View withdrawal statistics.\n- Suspend accounts where permitted.\n- Restore accounts where permitted.\n- Record administrative reasons.\n\nUse server-side pagination and filtering.\n\n## 13. Reward Management\n\nAllow administrators to inspect:\n- VEs.\n- SVEs.\n- Gems.\n- Tokens.\n- XP.\n- Related transactions.\n\nIf balance adjustments are implemented, require:\n- User ID.\n- Asset type.\n- Adjustment amount.\n- Previous balance.\n- New balance.\n- Reason.\n- Acting administrator.\n- Timestamp.\n- Transaction reference.\n\nNever allow silent direct balance overwrites.\n\nAll adjustments must create auditable transactions.\n\n## 14. Level and Achievement Management\n\nAllow administrators to manage supported configurations:\n\nLevels:\n- Level name.\n- XP threshold.\n- Display order.\n- Badge reference.\n- Active/inactive status.\n\nAchievements:\n- Achievement name.\n- Description.\n- Badge artwork.\n- Unlock conditions.\n- XP requirements.\n- Active/inactive status.\n\nThe backend must validate configurations and enforce actual unlock rules.\n\n## 15. Withdrawal Management\n\nAllow authorized administrators to:\n- Search withdrawal requests.\n- Filter by status.\n- View request details.\n- Approve requests.\n- Reject requests.\n- Record rejection reasons.\n- View status history.\n- Inspect related wallet transactions.\n\nFollow valid withdrawal status transitions and existing payout rules.\n\nDo not treat a pending request as a completed withdrawal.\n\n## 16. Referral Management\n\nAllow administrators to:\n- Search referral records.\n- View referral status.\n- Inspect reward records.\n- Review referral activity.\n- Investigate suspicious records where supported.\n\nAll changes must be validated and recorded.\n\n## 17. Activity and Audit Logs\n\nCreate audit logs for:\n- Admin login.\n- Profile modifications.\n- Account suspension/restoration.\n- Wallet adjustments.\n- Level configuration changes.\n- Achievement configuration changes.\n- Withdrawal status changes.\n- Referral administration.\n\nEach log must include:\n- Acting administrator.\n- Action.\n- Affected record.\n- Timestamp.\n- Relevant reason or change summary.\n\nNever log passwords or full authentication tokens.\n\n---\n\n# PART D \342\200\223 UI/UX DESIGN\n\n## 18. Design Direction\n\nCreate a premium, modern fintech and rewards-platform design.\n\nUse the existing VELoop background color:\n\n`#161827`\n\nUse a controlled palette:\n- Deep navy.\n- Soft blue.\n- Gold.\n- Warm yellow.\n- Muted purple.\n- Silver.\n- White.\n- Subtle green for success states.\n\nDesign requirements:\n- Premium and professional.\n- Clean and modern.\n- Visually engaging.\n- Gamified without appearing childish.\n- Easy to understand.\n- Mobile-first.\n- Accessible.\n- Fast and responsive.\n\nAvoid:\n- Excessive neon.\n- Rainbow gradients.\n- Casino-style effects.\n- Excessive glassmorphism.\n- Flashing animations.\n- Unnecessary decorative elements.\n\n## 19. Responsive Design\n\nSupport:\n- Mobile: 320px and above.\n- Tablet: 768px and above.\n- Laptop: 1280px and above.\n- Desktop: 1440px and above.\n- Large displays: 1920px and above.\n\nEnsure:\n- No horizontal overflow.\n- Readable text.\n- Proper spacing.\n- Responsive grids.\n- Accessible buttons.\n- Easy-to-use mobile navigation.\n- Responsive tables.\n- Appropriate tap targets.\n\n## 20. UI States\n\nCreate proper:\n- Loading skeletons.\n- Empty states.\n- Error states.\n- Retry buttons.\n- Success notifications.\n- Confirmation dialogs.\n\nNever show a blank page while data is loading.\n\nNever silently replace failed API data with hardcoded values.\n\n## 21. Interactions\n\nImplement:\n- Copy User ID.\n- Copy Referral Code.\n- Tooltips.\n- Progress bar animations.\n- Quick action navigation.\n- Search and filters.\n- Pagination.\n- Admin confirmation dialogs.\n- Toast notifications.\n- Responsive navigation.\n\nUse lightweight animations and support reduced-motion preferences.\n\n---\n\n# PART E \342\200\223 SECURITY AND TESTING\n\n## 22. Security Requirements\n\nImplement:\n- Secure password hashing.\n- Authentication middleware.\n- Admin authorization middleware.\n- Request validation.\n- Rate limiting.\n- Appropriate CORS configuration.\n- Environment variables.\n- HTTPS in deployment.\n- Secure session/token handling.\n- Protection against unauthorized data access.\n- Appropriate database indexes.\n- Safe error responses.\n\nNever commit:\n- `.env` files.\n- MongoDB credentials.\n- JWT secrets.\n- Real admin passwords.\n- Production API keys.\n\nCreate `.env.example` with placeholder values.\n\n## 23. Testing Requirements\n\nTest the following.\n\n### User tests\n- Profile loads from MongoDB-backed APIs.\n- Profile works after refreshing.\n- Logout and login preserve correct data.\n- Reward balances are accurate.\n- XP and level progression are correct.\n- Achievements display correct status.\n- Withdrawal totals are accurate.\n- Referral statistics are accurate.\n- Copy buttons work.\n- Loading and empty states work.\n\n### Security tests\n- User cannot access another user\'s profile.\n- User cannot modify their wallet balance.\n- User cannot submit fake XP.\n- User cannot change their level.\n- User cannot unlock achievements directly.\n- User cannot change withdrawal status.\n- Normal users cannot access admin APIs.\n- Invalid and expired credentials are rejected.\n\n### Admin tests\n- Admin login works.\n- Demo credentials work.\n- Dashboard statistics come from backend data.\n- User search and filters work.\n- Administrative changes persist.\n- Withdrawal status transitions are validated.\n- Audit logs are recorded.\n- Unauthorized actions are rejected.\n\n---\n\n# PART F \342\200\223 DOCUMENTATION AND DEPLOYMENT\n\n## 24. Required Documentation\n\nGenerate these files:\n\n### README.md\nInclude:\n- Project overview.\n- Features.\n- Technology stack.\n- Architecture.\n- Installation instructions.\n- Development commands.\n- Deployment instructions.\n- Live links.\n- Screenshots.\n- Environment variables.\n\n### API_DOCUMENTATION.md\nInclude:\n- User APIs.\n- Admin APIs.\n- HTTP methods.\n- Authentication requirements.\n- Request and response examples.\n- Error codes.\n\n### DATABASE.md\nInclude:\n- MongoDB models.\n- Relationships.\n- Indexes.\n- Data ownership.\n- Wallet integration.\n\n### SECURITY.md\nInclude:\n- Authentication.\n- Authorization.\n- Admin protection.\n- Validation.\n- Sensitive data protection.\n\n### ADMIN_GUIDE.md\nInclude:\n- Admin login.\n- Dashboard.\n- User management.\n- Reward management.\n- Withdrawal review.\n- Audit logs.\n\n### TESTING.md\nInclude:\n- Test scenarios.\n- Expected outcomes.\n- Actual results.\n- Known limitations.\n\n### DEMO_ACCESS.md\nInclude:\n- Demo admin URL.\n- Working demo admin credentials.\n- Optional demo user credentials.\n- Setup and reset instructions.\n\nAlso generate:\n- Postman collection.\n- `.env.example`.\n- Screenshots of mobile, tablet and desktop.\n- Screenshots of the admin dashboard.\n- A short demonstration video.\n\n## 25. Deployment\n\nDeploy:\n\nFrontend \342\206\222 Vercel\n\nBackend \342\206\222 Render or Railway\n\nDatabase \342\206\222 MongoDB Atlas\n\nEnsure:\n- Frontend communicates with deployed backend.\n- All environment variables are configured.\n- MongoDB connection works.\n- Authentication works in production/demo environment.\n- `/user-profile` works after refreshing.\n- Admin routes are protected.\n- No localhost URLs remain in production configuration.\n\n---\n\n# PART G \342\200\223 DEVELOPMENT WORKFLOW\n\n## 26. Follow This Development Order\n\nDo not skip phases.\n\n### Phase 1: Project Planning\n- Understand the complete assignment.\n- Plan the architecture.\n- Define frontend pages.\n- Define backend APIs.\n- Design database schemas.\n- Identify dependencies.\n- Create the folder structure.\n\nFirst show me the implementation plan.\n\n### Phase 2: UI/UX Design\n- Create the user profile layout.\n- Create the admin dashboard layout.\n- Design reusable components.\n- Implement responsive styling.\n- Verify desktop and mobile layouts.\n\n### Phase 3: Backend\n- Initialize Node.js and Express.\n- Connect MongoDB Atlas.\n- Create Mongoose models.\n- Build controllers and routes.\n- Add validation and error handling.\n- Implement authentication.\n\n### Phase 4: User Profile Integration\n- Connect React to backend APIs.\n- Fetch profile information.\n- Fetch reward balances.\n- Fetch level progress.\n- Fetch achievements.\n- Fetch withdrawals.\n- Fetch referrals.\n- Fetch recent activity.\n\n### Phase 5: Admin Panel\n- Implement admin login.\n- Build the dashboard.\n- Implement user management.\n- Implement reward management.\n- Implement withdrawal management.\n- Implement referral management.\n- Implement audit logs.\n\n### Phase 6: Security\n- Test authentication.\n- Test authorization.\n- Test user ownership.\n- Test wallet protection.\n- Test admin protection.\n- Fix vulnerabilities.\n\n### Phase 7: Testing\n- Test all features.\n- Test mobile responsiveness.\n- Test empty database states.\n- Test failed API requests.\n- Fix console errors.\n- Fix backend errors.\n\n### Phase 8: Deployment\n- Deploy frontend.\n- Deploy backend.\n- Configure MongoDB Atlas.\n- Configure environment variables.\n- Test live URLs.\n\n### Phase 9: Final Submission\n- Generate documentation.\n- Prepare Postman collection.\n- Prepare screenshots.\n- Prepare demo credentials.\n- Record demonstration video.\n- Verify all deliverables.\n\n---\n\n# PART H \342\200\223 IMPORTANT INSTRUCTIONS FOR THE AI CODING ASSISTANT\n\nFollow these rules throughout development:\n\n1. Do not generate a frontend-only project.\n2. Do not use hardcoded balances or fictional user statistics as real account information.\n3. Do not create fake admin functionality.\n4. Do not create buttons that do nothing.\n5. Do not invent VELoop business rules or existing platform APIs.\n6. If the existing platform\'s APIs or business rules are unavailable, clearly identify the missing information and create a clearly labelled development/demo implementation rather than pretending to integrate with production.\n7. Do not use production credentials.\n8. Never expose secrets in frontend code.\n9. Write reusable components.\n10. Use CSS Modules for component-specific styling.\n11. Keep frontend and backend code separate.\n12. Use proper error handling.\n13. Test every major feature before moving to the next phase.\n14. Do not silently skip incomplete features.\n15. Do not claim deployment or testing succeeded unless it has actually been verified.\n16. Explain important code in beginner-friendly language.\n17. Give exact terminal commands and specify which directory to execute them in.\n18. When changing an existing file, show the complete corrected file or a precise patch.\n19. Check that imports, routes, environment variables and API URLs are consistent.\n20. Maintain a checklist of completed and pending requirements.\n\n## 27. How You Should Work With Me\n\nI want to understand the application while building it.\n\nFor every development phase:\n\n- First explain what we are building.\n- Explain why it is needed.\n- List the files that will be created or modified.\n- Give the code in manageable sections.\n- Explain important functions and components.\n- Give exact commands to run.\n- Tell me how to test the result.\n- Wait for me to confirm that it works before proceeding to the next phase.\n\nIf an error occurs:\n- Read the error carefully.\n- Identify its actual cause.\n- Explain the problem simply.\n- Give the corrected code.\n- Do not randomly change unrelated files.\n\nDo not move ahead if the current phase has critical errors.\n\n## 28. First Task\n\nStart with **Phase 1: Project Planning**.\n\nBefore writing application code, provide:\n\n1. Complete project architecture.\n2. Frontend page structure.\n3. Backend folder structure.\n4. Database schema design.\n5. API endpoint list.\n6. Authentication flow.\n7. Admin authorization flow.\n8. UI/UX design plan.\n9. Development milestones.\n10. List of information that must be confirmed about the existing VELoop platform.\n\nThen wait for my approval before starting implementation.\n\n**Final goal:** Deliver a complete, secure, responsive, premium-quality MERN application with a working User Profile Dashboard and protected Admin Panel, backed by MongoDB and ready for evaluation."
    items {
      text: "# MASTER PROMPT: VELoop Rewards \342\200\223 Complete Full-Stack User Profile & Admin Management System\n\n## 1. Your Role\n\nAct as a senior full-stack MERN developer, UI/UX designer, database architect, security engineer, and technical mentor.\n\nI want you to build a complete, production-quality full-stack web application named **VELoop Rewards \342\200\223 User Profile & Admin Management System**.\n\nThis is a real internship assignment. It must be a functional application, not just a frontend prototype or static dashboard.\n\nYou are responsible for:\n- Planning the architecture.\n- Designing the UI/UX.\n- Building the React frontend.\n- Building the Node.js and Express backend.\n- Designing MongoDB schemas.\n- Implementing authentication and authorization.\n- Connecting all frontend pages to backend APIs.\n- Building a protected Admin Panel.\n- Testing and fixing errors.\n- Deploying the application.\n- Preparing technical documentation.\n\n**Important:** I am a beginner in full-stack development. Write clean, understandable code. Explain what each major file does, why a particular technology is used, and how different components communicate.\n\nDo not generate the entire project in one response. Work in small, manageable phases.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Mandatory Technology Stack\n\nUse the following technologies without replacing them with unrelated platforms.\n\n### Frontend\n- React.js\n- Vite\n- JavaScript\n- Bootstrap\n- CSS Modules (`.module.css`)\n- Lucide React\n- React Icons\n- React Router DOM\n- Axios or Fetch API\n\n### Backend\n- Node.js\n- Express.js\n- JavaScript\n- MongoDB\n- Mongoose\n- JWT-based authentication or an equally secure session-based approach\n- bcrypt for password hashing\n- Express middleware\n- Server-side validation\n\n### Deployment\n- Frontend: Vercel\n- Backend: Render or Railway\n- Database: MongoDB Atlas\n- Source control: GitHub\n\nDo not use Firebase, Supabase, or a static JSON file as a replacement for the required backend and database.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Project Structure\n\nCreate a clean and modular project structure.\n\n```text\nveloop-user-profile/\n\342\224\202\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 frontend/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 public/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 src/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 assets/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 components/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 ProfileHero/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 RewardAssets/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 LevelProgress/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 AchievementCard/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 WithdrawalOverview/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 ReferralSnapshot/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 RecentActivity/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 QuickActions/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 pages/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 UserProfile/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 Login/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 Admin/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202       \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 AdminLogin/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202       \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 Dashboard/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202       \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 Users/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202       \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 Rewards/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202       \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 Withdrawals/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202       \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 Referrals/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202       \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 Levels/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202       \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 Achievements/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202       \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 AuditLogs/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\202\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 services/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 context/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 hooks/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 utils/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 App.jsx\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 main.jsx\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202\n\342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 package.json\n\342\224\202\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 backend/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 src/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 config/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 controllers/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 middleware/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 models/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 routes/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 services/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 validators/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 utils/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 server.js\n\342\224\202   \342\224\202\n\342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 package.json\n\342\224\202\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 docs/\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 postman/\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 README.md\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 .gitignore\n\342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 .env.example\n```\n\nYou may improve the structure if necessary, but maintain modularity and separation of concerns.\n\n---\n\n# PART A \342\200\223 USER PROFILE APPLICATION\n\n## 4. User Profile Dashboard\n\nCreate a complete and visually attractive user profile page at:\n\n`/user-profile`\n\nThe profile page should work after refreshing the browser and after logging out and logging back in.\n\nCreate a new design. Do not simply copy an existing profile page.\n\n### A. Profile Hero Section\n\nDisplay:\n- User profile picture.\n- Initials fallback if there is no profile image.\n- Display name.\n- User ID.\n- Email.\n- Account status.\n- Current level.\n- Level name.\n- XP progress bar.\n- Profile settings button, only if the feature exists.\n\nExample layout:\n\n```text\nWelcome back, Alex!\n\nLevel 04 \342\200\223 Reward Explorer\n\n6,420 / 8,000 XP\n\n[Progress Bar]\n\n1,580 XP remaining to reach Level 05\n```\n\nThese are illustrative values only. Never use them as actual user information.\n\n### B. Personal Information\n\nDisplay:\n- User ID.\n- Full name.\n- Email address.\n- Referral code.\n- Account creation date.\n- Account status.\n- Current level.\n\nAdd copy buttons for User ID and Referral Code.\n\nShow a success toast when copied.\n\nNever display passwords, OTPs, JWT tokens, or private credentials.\n\n### C. Reward Assets\n\nCreate an attractive section called **Your Rewards**.\n\nDisplay five separate reward assets:\n\n1. VEs.\n2. SVEs.\n3. Gems.\n4. Tokens.\n5. XP.\n\nEach asset should have:\n- Its own icon.\n- Current balance.\n- Clear label.\n- Short explanation.\n- Tooltip or information icon.\n- Relevant action link if a real feature exists.\n\nUse distinct visual treatments for:\n- VEs.\n- Silver-themed SVEs.\n- Gems.\n- Tokens.\n- XP.\n\nImportant:\n- All balances must come from MongoDB-backed APIs or an approved existing wallet service.\n- Never hardcode balances.\n- XP is progression data, not redeemable wallet currency.\n- Do not invent conversion functionality.\n\n### D. Level Progress\n\nCreate a separate level progression section.\n\nDisplay:\n- Current level.\n- Current level name.\n- Current XP.\n- XP required for next level.\n- Remaining XP.\n- Progress percentage.\n- Next level preview.\n- Badge artwork, if available.\n\nThe backend must calculate and enforce level progression.\n\n### E. Achievements and Badges\n\nDisplay:\n- Unlocked achievements.\n- Achievement names.\n- Badge artwork.\n- Unlock status.\n- Next achievement.\n- Progress toward the next achievement.\n\nUse actual achievement records and backend rules.\n\nIf the platform does not support achievements yet, show an informative upcoming-feature or empty state. Do not invent achievements.\n\n### F. Withdrawal Overview\n\nCreate a withdrawal statistics section.\n\nDisplay:\n- Total withdrawal requests.\n- Pending requests.\n- Approved requests.\n- Rejected requests.\n- Failed requests, if supported.\n- Total successfully withdrawn amount.\n\nUse appropriate status labels:\n- Approved: green.\n- Pending: amber.\n- Rejected/failed: muted red.\n\nNever count pending withdrawals as successfully withdrawn.\n\n### G. Quick Actions\n\nCreate attractive interactive cards for supported actions, such as:\n- Earn More Rewards.\n- Get More Gems.\n- Redeem Rewards.\n- Watch Ads.\n- Refer Friends.\n- View Wallet.\n- Explore Features.\n- View Achievements.\n\nOnly link to real implemented routes or supported platform features.\n\n### H. Referral Snapshot\n\nDisplay:\n- Referral code.\n- Total referrals.\n- Successful referrals.\n- Pending referrals, if supported.\n- Referral rewards earned.\n- Referral XP.\n- Next milestone, if configured.\n\nAdd a copy referral code button.\n\nCalculate referral statistics from actual database records.\n\n### I. Recent Activity\n\nDisplay a list of recent activities, including supported events such as:\n- Reward earned.\n- XP earned.\n- Daily bonus.\n- Gem reward.\n- Referral reward.\n- Redemption request.\n- Completed withdrawal.\n\nEach activity should show:\n- Description.\n- Amount or asset.\n- Timestamp.\n- Status.\n\nUse persisted backend records, not fictional activity.\n\n### J. Account Information and Security\n\nShow:\n- Email.\n- User ID.\n- Member-since date.\n- Account status.\n- Email verification status, if tracked.\n- Relevant security information.\n\nDo not expose sensitive authentication information.\n\n---\n\n# PART B \342\200\223 BACKEND DEVELOPMENT\n\n## 5. Database Architecture\n\nUse MongoDB and Mongoose.\n\nCreate suitable models for:\n\n1. User\n2. Wallet\n3. WalletTransaction\n4. UserLevel / LevelConfiguration\n5. Achievement\n6. UserAchievement\n7. Referral\n8. Withdrawal / Redemption\n9. ActivityLog\n10. AdminUser\n11. AdminAuditLog\n\nDefine appropriate:\n- Schemas.\n- Data types.\n- Required fields.\n- Default values.\n- Validation.\n- Relationships.\n- Indexes.\n- Created and updated timestamps.\n\nAvoid duplicate sources of truth.\n\nFor example, do not create two independent wallet balances for the same user.\n\nIf an existing VELoop wallet service exists, integrate with its approved API rather than creating conflicting wallet data.\n\n## 6. Backend APIs\n\nCreate REST APIs for the user profile.\n\nSuggested endpoints:\n\n```text\nGET    /api/profile/me\nPATCH  /api/profile/me\n\nGET    /api/profile/rewards\nGET    /api/profile/level-progress\nGET    /api/profile/achievements\nGET    /api/profile/withdrawal-summary\nGET    /api/profile/referrals\nGET    /api/profile/activity\n```\n\nCreate authentication endpoints:\n\n```text\nPOST   /api/auth/register\nPOST   /api/auth/login\nPOST   /api/auth/logout\nGET    /api/auth/me\n```\n\nOnly implement registration if appropriate for the demo application.\n\nEvery protected API must:\n- Verify authentication.\n- Identify the current user from the verified session or token.\n- Check permissions.\n- Validate incoming data.\n- Return appropriate HTTP status codes.\n- Handle errors securely.\n\nNever trust a user ID supplied by the frontend to determine account ownership.\n\n## 7. Backend Data Integrity\n\nThe following values must only be controlled by the backend:\n\n- Wallet balances.\n- VEs.\n- SVEs.\n- Gems.\n- Tokens.\n- XP.\n- Level.\n- Achievement unlocks.\n- Withdrawal totals.\n- Referral rewards.\n- Account status.\n- Verification status.\n\nA user must not be able to modify these values through browser DevTools or API requests.\n\nIf users can edit their display name or other permitted profile fields, validate those fields separately.\n\nCreate centralized error handling and request validation.\n\n---\n\n# PART C \342\200\223 ADMIN PANEL\n\n## 8. Protected Admin Dashboard\n\nCreate a separate admin application section.\n\nSuggested routes:\n\n```text\n/admin/login\n/admin/dashboard\n/admin/users\n/admin/users/:id\n/admin/rewards\n/admin/levels\n/admin/achievements\n/admin/withdrawals\n/admin/referrals\n/admin/activity\n/admin/audit-logs\n/admin/settings\n```\n\nBuild a professional admin interface with:\n- Sidebar navigation.\n- Top navigation bar.\n- Search.\n- Filters.\n- Data tables.\n- Statistics cards.\n- Confirmation dialogs.\n- Notifications.\n- Responsive layout.\n\nThe Admin Panel must use real backend data.\n\nIt must not be a static dashboard.\n\n## 9. Admin Authentication\n\nImplement:\n- Admin login.\n- Secure logout.\n- Password hashing using bcrypt.\n- Session or token expiry.\n- Protected routes.\n- Role and permission checks.\n- Login rate limiting.\n- Protection against repeated failed login attempts.\n\nCreate a Super Admin role with authority over the implemented profile module.\n\nNever grant admin privileges based on a frontend role value or user-editable email.\n\n## 10. Demo Admin Credentials\n\nCreate a working development/demo admin account through a secure seed script.\n\nThe demo credentials must:\n- Actually work.\n- Be stored securely.\n- Be available to the reviewer.\n- Be documented in `DEMO_ACCESS.md`.\n\nDisplay a Demo Credentials panel on the admin login page only in the explicitly configured demo environment.\n\nNever expose production admin passwords or secrets in frontend code.\n\n## 11. Admin Dashboard\n\nDisplay actual backend statistics:\n\n- Total registered users.\n- Active users.\n- Suspended users, if supported.\n- Total wallet balances by asset.\n- Total withdrawal requests.\n- Pending withdrawals.\n- Approved withdrawals.\n- Completed withdrawals.\n- Referral statistics.\n- Recent activity.\n- Relevant system alerts.\n\nIf the database is empty, show an empty state rather than fabricated numbers.\n\n## 12. Admin User Management\n\nAllow administrators to:\n- Search users.\n- Filter users by account status.\n- View user profiles.\n- View wallet balances.\n- View transaction history.\n- View level and XP progress.\n- View achievements.\n- View referrals.\n- View withdrawal statistics.\n- Suspend accounts where permitted.\n- Restore accounts where permitted.\n- Record administrative reasons.\n\nUse server-side pagination and filtering.\n\n## 13. Reward Management\n\nAllow administrators to inspect:\n- VEs.\n- SVEs.\n- Gems.\n- Tokens.\n- XP.\n- Related transactions.\n\nIf balance adjustments are implemented, require:\n- User ID.\n- Asset type.\n- Adjustment amount.\n- Previous balance.\n- New balance.\n- Reason.\n- Acting administrator.\n- Timestamp.\n- Transaction reference.\n\nNever allow silent direct balance overwrites.\n\nAll adjustments must create auditable transactions.\n\n## 14. Level and Achievement Management\n\nAllow administrators to manage supported configurations:\n\nLevels:\n- Level name.\n- XP threshold.\n- Display order.\n- Badge reference.\n- Active/inactive status.\n\nAchievements:\n- Achievement name.\n- Description.\n- Badge artwork.\n- Unlock conditions.\n- XP requirements.\n- Active/inactive status.\n\nThe backend must validate configurations and enforce actual unlock rules.\n\n## 15. Withdrawal Management\n\nAllow authorized administrators to:\n- Search withdrawal requests.\n- Filter by status.\n- View request details.\n- Approve requests.\n- Reject requests.\n- Record rejection reasons.\n- View status history.\n- Inspect related wallet transactions.\n\nFollow valid withdrawal status transitions and existing payout rules.\n\nDo not treat a pending request as a completed withdrawal.\n\n## 16. Referral Management\n\nAllow administrators to:\n- Search referral records.\n- View referral status.\n- Inspect reward records.\n- Review referral activity.\n- Investigate suspicious records where supported.\n\nAll changes must be validated and recorded.\n\n## 17. Activity and Audit Logs\n\nCreate audit logs for:\n- Admin login.\n- Profile modifications.\n- Account suspension/restoration.\n- Wallet adjustments.\n- Level configuration changes.\n- Achievement configuration changes.\n- Withdrawal status changes.\n- Referral administration.\n\nEach log must include:\n- Acting administrator.\n- Action.\n- Affected record.\n- Timestamp.\n- Relevant reason or change summary.\n\nNever log passwords or full authentication tokens.\n\n---\n\n# PART D \342\200\223 UI/UX DESIGN\n\n## 18. Design Direction\n\nCreate a premium, modern fintech and rewards-platform design.\n\nUse the existing VELoop background color:\n\n`#161827`\n\nUse a controlled palette:\n- Deep navy.\n- Soft blue.\n- Gold.\n- Warm yellow.\n- Muted purple.\n- Silver.\n- White.\n- Subtle green for success states.\n\nDesign requirements:\n- Premium and professional.\n- Clean and modern.\n- Visually engaging.\n- Gamified without appearing childish.\n- Easy to understand.\n- Mobile-first.\n- Accessible.\n- Fast and responsive.\n\nAvoid:\n- Excessive neon.\n- Rainbow gradients.\n- Casino-style effects.\n- Excessive glassmorphism.\n- Flashing animations.\n- Unnecessary decorative elements.\n\n## 19. Responsive Design\n\nSupport:\n- Mobile: 320px and above.\n- Tablet: 768px and above.\n- Laptop: 1280px and above.\n- Desktop: 1440px and above.\n- Large displays: 1920px and above.\n\nEnsure:\n- No horizontal overflow.\n- Readable text.\n- Proper spacing.\n- Responsive grids.\n- Accessible buttons.\n- Easy-to-use mobile navigation.\n- Responsive tables.\n- Appropriate tap targets.\n\n## 20. UI States\n\nCreate proper:\n- Loading skeletons.\n- Empty states.\n- Error states.\n- Retry buttons.\n- Success notifications.\n- Confirmation dialogs.\n\nNever show a blank page while data is loading.\n\nNever silently replace failed API data with hardcoded values.\n\n## 21. Interactions\n\nImplement:\n- Copy User ID.\n- Copy Referral Code.\n- Tooltips.\n- Progress bar animations.\n- Quick action navigation.\n- Search and filters.\n- Pagination.\n- Admin confirmation dialogs.\n- Toast notifications.\n- Responsive navigation.\n\nUse lightweight animations and support reduced-motion preferences.\n\n---\n\n# PART E \342\200\223 SECURITY AND TESTING\n\n## 22. Security Requirements\n\nImplement:\n- Secure password hashing.\n- Authentication middleware.\n- Admin authorization middleware.\n- Request validation.\n- Rate limiting.\n- Appropriate CORS configuration.\n- Environment variables.\n- HTTPS in deployment.\n- Secure session/token handling.\n- Protection against unauthorized data access.\n- Appropriate database indexes.\n- Safe error responses.\n\nNever commit:\n- `.env` files.\n- MongoDB credentials.\n- JWT secrets.\n- Real admin passwords.\n- Production API keys.\n\nCreate `.env.example` with placeholder values.\n\n## 23. Testing Requirements\n\nTest the following.\n\n### User tests\n- Profile loads from MongoDB-backed APIs.\n- Profile works after refreshing.\n- Logout and login preserve correct data.\n- Reward balances are accurate.\n- XP and level progression are correct.\n- Achievements display correct status.\n- Withdrawal totals are accurate.\n- Referral statistics are accurate.\n- Copy buttons work.\n- Loading and empty states work.\n\n### Security tests\n- User cannot access another user\'s profile.\n- User cannot modify their wallet balance.\n- User cannot submit fake XP.\n- User cannot change their level.\n- User cannot unlock achievements directly.\n- User cannot change withdrawal status.\n- Normal users cannot access admin APIs.\n- Invalid and expired credentials are rejected.\n\n### Admin tests\n- Admin login works.\n- Demo credentials work.\n- Dashboard statistics come from backend data.\n- User search and filters work.\n- Administrative changes persist.\n- Withdrawal status transitions are validated.\n- Audit logs are recorded.\n- Unauthorized actions are rejected.\n\n---\n\n# PART F \342\200\223 DOCUMENTATION AND DEPLOYMENT\n\n## 24. Required Documentation\n\nGenerate these files:\n\n### README.md\nInclude:\n- Project overview.\n- Features.\n- Technology stack.\n- Architecture.\n- Installation instructions.\n- Development commands.\n- Deployment instructions.\n- Live links.\n- Screenshots.\n- Environment variables.\n\n### API_DOCUMENTATION.md\nInclude:\n- User APIs.\n- Admin APIs.\n- HTTP methods.\n- Authentication requirements.\n- Request and response examples.\n- Error codes.\n\n### DATABASE.md\nInclude:\n- MongoDB models.\n- Relationships.\n- Indexes.\n- Data ownership.\n- Wallet integration.\n\n### SECURITY.md\nInclude:\n- Authentication.\n- Authorization.\n- Admin protection.\n- Validation.\n- Sensitive data protection.\n\n### ADMIN_GUIDE.md\nInclude:\n- Admin login.\n- Dashboard.\n- User management.\n- Reward management.\n- Withdrawal review.\n- Audit logs.\n\n### TESTING.md\nInclude:\n- Test scenarios.\n- Expected outcomes.\n- Actual results.\n- Known limitations.\n\n### DEMO_ACCESS.md\nInclude:\n- Demo admin URL.\n- Working demo admin credentials.\n- Optional demo user credentials.\n- Setup and reset instructions.\n\nAlso generate:\n- Postman collection.\n- `.env.example`.\n- Screenshots of mobile, tablet and desktop.\n- Screenshots of the admin dashboard.\n- A short demonstration video.\n\n## 25. Deployment\n\nDeploy:\n\nFrontend \342\206\222 Vercel\n\nBackend \342\206\222 Render or Railway\n\nDatabase \342\206\222 MongoDB Atlas\n\nEnsure:\n- Frontend communicates with deployed backend.\n- All environment variables are configured.\n- MongoDB connection works.\n- Authentication works in production/demo environment.\n- `/user-profile` works after refreshing.\n- Admin routes are protected.\n- No localhost URLs remain in production configuration.\n\n---\n\n# PART G \342\200\223 DEVELOPMENT WORKFLOW\n\n## 26. Follow This Development Order\n\nDo not skip phases.\n\n### Phase 1: Project Planning\n- Understand the complete assignment.\n- Plan the architecture.\n- Define frontend pages.\n- Define backend APIs.\n- Design database schemas.\n- Identify dependencies.\n- Create the folder structure.\n\nFirst show me the implementation plan.\n\n### Phase 2: UI/UX Design\n- Create the user profile layout.\n- Create the admin dashboard layout.\n- Design reusable components.\n- Implement responsive styling.\n- Verify desktop and mobile layouts.\n\n### Phase 3: Backend\n- Initialize Node.js and Express.\n- Connect MongoDB Atlas.\n- Create Mongoose models.\n- Build controllers and routes.\n- Add validation and error handling.\n- Implement authentication.\n\n### Phase 4: User Profile Integration\n- Connect React to backend APIs.\n- Fetch profile information.\n- Fetch reward balances.\n- Fetch level progress.\n- Fetch achievements.\n- Fetch withdrawals.\n- Fetch referrals.\n- Fetch recent activity.\n\n### Phase 5: Admin Panel\n- Implement admin login.\n- Build the dashboard.\n- Implement user management.\n- Implement reward management.\n- Implement withdrawal management.\n- Implement referral management.\n- Implement audit logs.\n\n### Phase 6: Security\n- Test authentication.\n- Test authorization.\n- Test user ownership.\n- Test wallet protection.\n- Test admin protection.\n- Fix vulnerabilities.\n\n### Phase 7: Testing\n- Test all features.\n- Test mobile responsiveness.\n- Test empty database states.\n- Test failed API requests.\n- Fix console errors.\n- Fix backend errors.\n\n### Phase 8: Deployment\n- Deploy frontend.\n- Deploy backend.\n- Configure MongoDB Atlas.\n- Configure environment variables.\n- Test live URLs.\n\n### Phase 9: Final Submission\n- Generate documentation.\n- Prepare Postman collection.\n- Prepare screenshots.\n- Prepare demo credentials.\n- Record demonstration video.\n- Verify all deliverables.\n\n---\n\n# PART H \342\200\223 IMPORTANT INSTRUCTIONS FOR THE AI CODING ASSISTANT\n\nFollow these rules throughout development:\n\n1. Do not generate a frontend-only project.\n2. Do not use hardcoded balances or fictional user statistics as real account information.\n3. Do not create fake admin functionality.\n4. Do not create buttons that do nothing.\n5. Do not invent VELoop business rules or existing platform APIs.\n6. If the existing platform\'s APIs or business rules are unavailable, clearly identify the missing information and create a clearly labelled development/demo implementation rather than pretending to integrate with production.\n7. Do not use production credentials.\n8. Never expose secrets in frontend code.\n9. Write reusable components.\n10. Use CSS Modules for component-specific styling.\n11. Keep frontend and backend code separate.\n12. Use proper error handling.\n13. Test every major feature before moving to the next phase.\n14. Do not silently skip incomplete features.\n15. Do not claim deployment or testing succeeded unless it has actually been verified.\n16. Explain important code in beginner-friendly language.\n17. Give exact terminal commands and specify which directory to execute them in.\n18. When changing an existing file, show the complete corrected file or a precise patch.\n19. Check that imports, routes, environment variables and API URLs are consistent.\n20. Maintain a checklist of completed and pending requirements.\n\n## 27. How You Should Work With Me\n\nI want to understand the application while building it.\n\nFor every development phase:\n\n- First explain what we are building.\n- Explain why it is needed.\n- List the files that will be created or modified.\n- Give the code in manageable sections.\n- Explain important functions and components.\n- Give exact commands to run.\n- Tell me how to test the result.\n- Wait for me to confirm that it works before proceeding to the next phase.\n\nIf an error occurs:\n- Read the error carefully.\n- Identify its actual cause.\n- Explain the problem simply.\n- Give the corrected code.\n- Do not randomly change unrelated files.\n\nDo not move ahead if the current phase has critical errors.\n\n## 28. First Task\n\nStart with **Phase 1: Project Planning**.\n\nBefore writing application code, provide:\n\n1. Complete project architecture.\n2. Frontend page structure.\n3. Backend folder structure.\n4. Database schema design.\n5. API endpoint list.\n6. Authentication flow.\n7. Admin authorization flow.\n8. UI/UX design plan.\n9. Development milestones.\n10. List of information that must be confirmed about the existing VELoop platform.\n\nThen wait for my approval before starting implementation.\n\n**Final goal:** Deliver a complete, secure, responsive, premium-quality MERN application with a working User Profile Dashboard and protected Admin Panel, backed by MongoDB and ready for evaluation."
    }
  }
}
